Hi Dustin,

Thanks for the suggestion. Unfortunately, I have a really nasty non-linear component which cycles on an irregular basis. Unless I ignore the underlying assumptions, I can't fit a linear model to the data. The other alternative is to ignore the serial dependence, chop the data into a series of matched pairs (one at each time-step) and to run a sensitivity analysis on a Wilcoxon Matched Pairs test, as in rbounds. Either way, ignoring the underlying structure of the data seems to risk inflating Type I error, and finding the estimates of impact overly sensitive to a hidden bias.  In theory I could report this as the maximum sensitivity to hidden bias, acknowledging the (unknown) probability of a false positive but I suspect reviewers will foam at the mouth at this approach....

Thanks so much for the response, very much appreciated!

Louise



